\NewPage
\ReportEntry{06\quad Model-based forecasting of cohort trajectories}
\PageTitle{06 / THE FORECAST MODEL}{Model-based forecasting\newline of cohort trajectories}{Completed history anchors a projection of the first twelve monthly payments.}
\textbf{Renewal.} Customers differ in their tendency to cancel. Those more likely to leave tend to go first, so a cohort's churn can fall as it ages. The model estimates this pattern by stated need, channel and joining month, letting young cohorts borrow evidence from older ones. This extends the retention approach of \SourceCite{fader2007}{Fader and Hardie (2007)}.

\textbf{Spending and contribution.} A revenue model adapted from \SourceCite{orduz2024}{Orduz (2024)} estimates spending per remaining member. Recorded refund and variable-cost relationships convert revenue into contribution. Actual outcomes stay as recorded; only the remaining months are forecast.

\ReportFigure{retention-forecast}{A common horizon for cohorts with different histories. Solid points are observed; dashed lines extend the cohort to twelve payments. Examples are selected by the amount of observed history.}{retention-forecast.pdf}{3.1in}
\Section{How much weight to put on a forecast}
A young cohort has less evidence of its own. The next page therefore shows the forecast share alongside value, making that dependence visible. Historical checks against withheld outcomes favored the model over its simple baseline; Appendix D reports the errors by test and history length.

Shading represents parameter uncertainty in the expected path. Future behavior and cost relationships may change; the decision should be revisited as the new cohorts' renewal and contribution become observable.

